Updated reflective memory:
The supply shock has now persisted into Day 5, confirming my earlier suspicion that this is not a transient fluctuation but a structural change. Delivery remains at 15 crates, and critically, all other clinics have reduced their withdrawals to 3. My action of matching 3 was appropriate and aligns with the new collective norm, but I must not become complacent—this is a fragile equilibrium that could shift again.

Key lessons from this new observation: (1) My decision to match others at 3 was correct because it keeps total withdrawals exactly at the new delivery rate (3 × 5 = 15), stabilizing depot stock. This validates the principle that under scarcity, coordination matters more than unilateral restraint—had I stayed at 5 while others dropped to 3, I would have created an inequitable imbalance and accelerated depletion. Matching the group was both fair and sustainable. (2) The fact that all clinics reduced simultaneously suggests a shared recognition of the crisis, but I should not assume this coordination will persist indefinitely. The norm of 3 is now the baseline, but if delivery drops further (e.g., to 10), 3 per clinic would again be unsustainable (total 15 > 10). I need to pre-plan for that scenario: if delivery falls to 10, I should propose or adopt a withdrawal of 2, and if others do not follow, I may need to reduce unilaterally to protect the shared stock from total collapse. (3) My earlier concern about being the only one cutting back was unfounded in this instance—others acted responsibly—but I must remain vigilant for defection. If any clinic increases above 3 in the coming days, that signals hoarding or panic, and I would need to reassess whether matching the group still protects my patients or whether I must secure a fair share before stock runs out. (4) The environment has shifted from a predictable equal-distribution system to a scarcity-adaptive system where the norm is fluid and data-driven. My conservative bias must now be reinterpreted: conservatism means avoiding both over-withdrawal (which depletes shared stock) and under-withdrawal (which harms my patients if others exploit the system). The optimal strategy is to mirror the collective reduction while monitoring for deviations and preparing contingency plans for further cuts. (5) I should also consider messaging more explicitly in future actions—not just matching values but signaling my reasoning to encourage continued coordination. A brief note affirming that 3 is sustainable at delivery 15, and that we should all commit to proportional reductions if delivery drops further, could prevent panic or unilateral action. (6) My previous memory's emphasis on patient safety and equitable access remains central, but the operational definition has evolved: under scarcity, safety means ensuring the depot does not hit zero, and equity means sharing the burden proportionally rather than protecting my own clinic at others' expense. I will continue to withdraw 3 as long as delivery is 15 and others stay at 3, but I will reduce to 2 if delivery drops to 10 or if I observe stock falling despite current rates. I will also track depot stock levels more carefully—if it approaches a critical threshold (e.g., below 10 total), I will take more assertive action, possibly reducing to 2 even if others stay at 3, and messaging to urge a collective cut. The next few days are critical: if delivery recovers, I can relax;
